\subsection{Global enrichment cache}
\label{sec:enrichment-cache}

Fetching references and citations is expensive---it requires API calls that count
against rate limits. When the same paper appears in different searches or sessions,
we don't want to re-fetch its refs/cites. This section implements a global cache
that persists enrichment data across sessions.

The cache stores refs/cites keyed by paper ID (using the same [[get_paper_id]]
function from the notes module). We store in the user's data directory (not cache
directory) because this data represents work done and is expensive to regenerate.

The data directory itself comes from [[get_data_dir]] in the notes module,
the one place that decides where scholar keeps its data.
This module used to read [[SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR]] and ask [[platformdirs]]
itself, a second copy of that decision which a change of the rule (say, a
new override variable) would have had to find and update too, and which
nothing would have flagged had it been missed: the enrichment cache would
quietly have stayed in the old place.
The notes module is already imported for [[get_paper_id]], so delegating
adds no dependency, and the notes module imports nothing from here that
could close a cycle.

<<enrichment cache imports>>=
import json
from dataclasses import replace
from pathlib import Path

from scholar.notes import get_data_dir, get_paper_id
@

<<enrichment functions>>=
def _get_enrichment_cache_dir() -> Path:
    """
    Return the directory for storing enriched paper data.

    A subdirectory of the data directory (`SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR` if set,
    otherwise the platform's), created if it doesn't exist.
    """
    path = get_data_dir() / ENRICHMENT_CACHE_DIR
    path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return path
@

Let's verify that the enrichment cache follows the data directory
wherever [[get_data_dir]] puts it, not only when [[SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR]] is
set: we move the data directory by replacing the function, which a
private copy of the rule would not notice.

