\subsection{Counting the outcomes}

The command wants a tally, not a list of outcomes, and the tally is
easier to test away from the command.
[[tally_lookups]] counts, over the papers a run processed, those still
without an abstract, and sorts them into four kinds:
\begin{description}
\item[Answered] every provider asked answered, and none had an
  abstract: there is none in any database asked.
\item[Failed] at least one lookup failed; these are counted again per
  provider and reason, one count per paper, so a paper both Semantic
  Scholar and OpenAlex refused counts once in each row.
\item[Without a DOI] nothing could be asked.
\item[None asked] the paper has a DOI, but no provider was asked:
  each declined, or none is configured.
\end{description}
A paper is answered when every provider that was asked answered;
providers that declined do not count against it.
They are reported apart, one line per provider and reason with the
number of papers still without an abstract that it declined for, and
without the hint to run again.
A paper whose lookups failed but which got its abstract elsewhere is
not counted: the failure cost it nothing.

The open-access status gets the same treatment over the papers whose
status is still unknown after the run: without a DOI, not in Unpaywall
(it answered, but with no record), or a failed lookup, counted per
reason.
A paper that has a status from an earlier run keeps it when the lookup
fails ([[annotate_paper]] leaves it unchanged), so it is not counted.

The outcomes are matched to the papers by position, as
[[enrich_papers]] appends them; a paper without an outcome, because
the caller did not collect one, reads as one with nothing asked.

